Spring Boot auto‑configuration is a feature that automatically configures Spring beans based on the libraries that are present on the classpath. It eliminates the need for boiler‑plate `@Configuration` classes for common setups such as JDBC, JPA, MVC, etc.

## How it works
1. **Starter JARs** bundle `@Configuration` classes that are annotated with conditional annotations such as `@ConditionalOnClass`, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, etc.
2. Each starter declares its auto‑configuration classes in `META-INF/spring.factories`.
3. `@SpringBootApplication` triggers `SpringApplication.run()`. During startup, Spring Boot loads all auto‑configuration classes, evaluates the conditions, and registers the beans that are missing.

## Demo: Auto‑Config for a DataSource
```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
</dependency>
```
```properties
# src/main/resources/application.properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
```
Run the app:
```
$ mvn spring-boot:run
```
Spring Boot will:
- Load `DataSourceAutoConfiguration`, `HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration`, etc.
- Create a `DataSource` bean (HikariCP by default).
- Create an `EntityManagerFactory` bean.

To verify:
```java
@Component
public class DataSourceVerifier implements CommandLineRunner {
    private final DataSource ds;
    public DataSourceVerifier(DataSource ds) { this.ds = ds; }
    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        System.out.println("Auto‑configured DataSource: " + ds.getClass().getName());
    }
}
```
Output:
```
Auto‑configured DataSource: com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource
```

## Disabling auto‑configuration
```java
@SpringBootApplication(exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
public class DemoApplication { ... }
```
Now you must define the bean yourself.

## Overriding an auto‑configured bean
```java
@Configuration
public class CustomJdbcTemplateConfig {
    @Bean
    public JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate(DataSource ds) {
        JdbcTemplate jt = new JdbcTemplate(ds);
        jt.setResultsMapCaseInsensitive(true);
        return jt;
    }
}
```

## Query‑Based Example: `@ConfigurationProperties`
```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app")
public class AppConfig {
    private String name;
    private String version;
    private boolean debug;
    // getters/setters
}
```
Register and consume:
```java
@Configuration
@EnableConfigurationProperties(AppConfig.class)
public class AppConfigRegistrar { }

@Service
public class AppInfoService {
    private final AppConfig cfg;
    public AppInfoService(AppConfig cfg) { this.cfg = cfg; }
    public String getInfo() {
        return String.format("%s v%s (debug=%b)", cfg.getName(), cfg.getVersion(), cfg.isDebug());
    }
}
```
With `application.properties`:
```
app.name=DemoApp
app.version=1.0.0
app.debug=true
```
Spring Boot automatically binds these properties to `AppConfig`.

## TL;DR
- Auto‑configuration = “If I have this library on my classpath, set up the usual beans for me.”
- Starter JARs provide the needed config classes.
- Conditional annotations guard against missing dependencies or user overrides.
- `@ConfigurationProperties` provides type‑safe external configuration.
- Profiles can enable/disable specific auto‑configurations.

